{"state_code":"CT",
"policies":[
{"id":"CT-local_preemption-1","technology":"solar;wind;battery_storage","policy_type":"local_preemption","who_decides":"state_board","state_body":"Connecticut Siting Council","threshold_mw":"solar:1;wind:1","summary":"For facilities under its jurisdiction the Siting Council decides location and type, gives municipal regulations only the weight it deems appropriate, and its certificate replaces state and municipal approvals on public need. Town zoning commissions and inland wetland agencies may issue orders restricting a proposed generating facility within 65 days of the application, but the Council may affirm, modify or revoke those orders by a vote of six members.","statute_citation":"Conn. Gen. Stat. 16-50x(a),(d)","statute_url":"https://www.cga.ct.gov/current/pub/chap_277a.htm#sec_16-50x","evidence_level":"primary_source","verification":"verified","reviewed_on":"2026-10-09"},
{"id":"CT-other-1","technology":"solar","policy_type":"other","who_decides":"state_board","state_body":"Connecticut Siting Council","threshold_mw":"solar:2","summary":"A solar photovoltaic facility of 2 MW or more on prime farmland or forestland may be approved by declaratory ruling only if the Department of Agriculture states in writing that it will not materially affect the land as prime farmland, or the Department of Energy and Environmental Protection states that it will not materially affect the land as core forest, and the applicant posts a bond for decommissioning and restoring the farmland soils.","statute_citation":"Conn. Gen. Stat. 16-50k(a)(B)(iii) (P.A. 17-218, sec. 3; P.A. 23-163, sec. 1; P.A. 25-127, sec. 1)","statute_url":"https://www.cga.ct.gov/2026/sup/chap_277a.htm#sec_16-50k","evidence_level":"primary_source","verification":"verified","reviewed_on":"2026-10-09"},
{"id":"CT-siting_authority-1","technology":"solar;wind;battery_storage","policy_type":"siting_authority","who_decides":"state_board","state_body":"Connecticut Siting Council","threshold_mw":"solar:1;wind:1","summary":"The Connecticut Siting Council has exclusive jurisdiction over the location and type of electric generating and storage facilities; only renewable plants of 1 MW or less owned by a private power producer that is a federal qualifying facility (or built mainly for own use) fall outside it and are left to local zoning. Projects that may have a substantial adverse environmental effect need a certificate of environmental compatibility and public need, while distributed resource projects up to 65 MW are approved by declaratory ruling.","statute_citation":"Conn. Gen. Stat. 16-50i(a)(3), 16-50k(a), 16-50x(a)","statute_url":"https://www.cga.ct.gov/2026/sup/chap_277a.htm#sec_16-50k","evidence_level":"primary_source","verification":"verified","reviewed_on":"2026-10-09"},
{"id":"CT-state_moratorium-1","technology":"solar","policy_type":"state_moratorium","who_decides":"state_board","state_body":"Connecticut Siting Council","threshold_mw":null,"summary":"Until July 1, 2027, the Siting Council may not approve a solar photovoltaic facility, by certificate or declaratory ruling, in a town where solar already approved by the Council covers more than 5.5 percent of the town's land (or more than 2 percent in a town that borders such a town and lies to its north), measured by total parcel size and not counting solar on brownfields or landfills. The bar does not apply to sites zoned commercial or industrial as of January 1, 2024, and a state report on spreading solar siting more evenly is due February 1, 2027.","statute_citation":"2026 Conn. Pub. Acts 26-127, sec. 16 (HB 5340; uncodified)","statute_url":"https://www.cga.ct.gov/2026/ACT/PA/PDF/2026PA-00127-R00HB-05340-PA.PDF","evidence_level":"primary_source","verification":"verified","reviewed_on":"2026-10-09"},
{"id":"CT-state_setback_standard-1","technology":"wind","policy_type":"state_setback_standard","who_decides":"state_board","state_body":"Connecticut Siting Council","threshold_mw":null,"summary":"Siting Council rules require wind turbines to be set back from all property lines at least 2.5 times turbine height for projects over 65 MW (certificate) or 1.5 times height for smaller projects (declaratory ruling), or the manufacturer distance if greater; setbacks may be waived by neighbor consent or a two-thirds Council vote but never below 1.5 times height from an occupied home. Shadow flicker is capped at 30 hours a year at any occupied structure, and noise must meet state noise control rules.","statute_citation":"Conn. Agencies Regs. 16-50j-95(a)-(c); see also 16-50j-92 to 16-50j-94","statute_url":"https://eregulations.ct.gov/eRegsPortal/Browse/RCSA/Title_16Subtitle_16-50jSection_16-50j-95/","evidence_level":"primary_source","verification":"verified","reviewed_on":"2026-10-09"},
{"id":"CT-state_setback_standard-2","technology":"solar","policy_type":"state_setback_standard","who_decides":"state_board","state_body":"Connecticut Siting Council","threshold_mw":null,"summary":"The Siting Council may not grant a certificate for a solar photovoltaic facility if it finds that any inverter or transformer would be less than 200 feet from the property line, or that the facility would not meet the state noise rules adopted under the noise pollution control chapter (chapter 442). The limit applies to certificates; the statute does not extend it to projects approved by declaratory ruling.","statute_citation":"Conn. Gen. Stat. 16-50p(c)(5) (P.A. 24-144, sec. 5); see also 16-50p(a)(3)(H)","statute_url":"https://www.cga.ct.gov/current/pub/chap_277a.htm#sec_16-50p","evidence_level":"primary_source","verification":"verified","reviewed_on":"2026-10-09"}
],
